SOURCE
id: 1
ts: 2026-05-29T00:00:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: A Complexity Measure
authors: Thomas J. McCabe
year: 1976
venue: IEEE Transactions on Software Engineering, Vol. SE-2, No. 4, pp. 308-320
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1109/TSE.1976.233837
keywords: cyclomatic complexity, control flow graph, basis paths, structured programming, V(G)
cites:
cited_by: Essential complexity (McCabe follow-on), NPATH (Nejmeh 1988), virtually all subsequent complexity metric work
content:
  PRIMARY METRIC: Cyclomatic Complexity V(G)
  Formula: V(G) = E - N + 2P
    E = number of edges in the control-flow graph
    N = number of nodes in the control-flow graph
    P = number of connected components (P=1 for a single function/subroutine)
    For a single connected component: V(G) = E - N + 2
  
  ALTERNATIVE FORMULA (for strongly-connected graph, exit looped back to entry):
    V(G) = E - N + P
  
  ALTERNATIVE FORMULA (decision-point method):
    V(G) = number of binary decision points + 1
    (at machine-instruction level; each predicate = 1 binary decision)
  
  ALGEBRAIC INTERPRETATION:
    V(G) = dimension of the cycle space of G over GF(2)
    V(G) = first Betti number = rank H_1(G, t)
  
  MEANING: Number of linearly independent paths through the CFG;
    upper bound on number of test cases for branch coverage;
    lower bound on number of paths.
  
  CFG CONSTRUCTION: Each node = sequence of non-branching statements.
    Decision points split into two edges (if-then → two outgoing edges).
    Exit node has back-edge to entry node to make graph strongly connected.
  
  T1-T2 REDUCTION (cited in later works on essential complexity):
    T1: remove self-loop on a node (reduces V by 0, cleans graph)
    T2: replace a node with exactly one predecessor and one successor by a
        single edge (collapses sequential sequences)
    These reductions simulate structured programming condensation.
  
  RISK THRESHOLDS (McCabe's original categorization):
    V(G) 1-10:  Simple, little risk
    V(G) 11-20: Moderate complexity and risk
    V(G) 21-50: Complex, high risk
    V(G) > 50:  Untestable code, very high risk

  EXTENSION - MULTIPLE EXIT POINTS:
    V(G) = pi - s + 2  (pi = decision points, s = number of exit points)
  
  CC2 (extended): CC2 = V(G) + Boolean operators (each And/Or inside condition)
  CC3 (modified): Treats entire switch/select-case as one decision (not per case)
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 2
ts: 2026-05-29T00:00:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Essential Complexity (derived from McCabe 1976)
authors: Thomas J. McCabe
year: 1976
venue: IEEE Transactions on Software Engineering, Vol. SE-2, No. 4 (same paper as above)
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Essential_complexity
keywords: essential complexity, unstructured code, CFG reduction, goto, spaghetti code, structured programming, irreducible graph
cites: McCabe 1976
cited_by: GNAT compiler metrics, static analysis tools for unstructuredness
content:
  DEFINITION: Essential complexity ev(G) = cyclomatic complexity of the reduced CFG
    after iteratively removing ALL structured control structures (single-entry/
    single-exit subgraphs) by replacing them with placeholder single nodes.
  
  REDUCTION RULE: If a subgraph has exactly one entry edge and one exit edge
    (i.e., it is a "D-chart" or "structured" fragment), replace it with a
    single node. Repeat until no more reductions are possible.
    This is a graph transformation independent of program inputs.
  
  STRUCTURED CONTROL STRUCTURES THAT GET REDUCED:
    - if-then-else (single entry, single exit)
    - while loop (single entry, single exit)
    - do-while loop
    - for loop
    - try-catch-finally blocks (if structured)
  
  RESULT INTERPRETATION:
    ev(G) = 1: Program is fully structured; the entire CFG reduces to a single node.
              All structured programs reduce to 1.
    ev(G) > 1: Program contains irreducible subgraphs (unstructured code).
              Higher values = more "spaghetti"; more goto/unstructured jumps.
    ev(G) = ev_max (= V(G)): Completely unstructured; nothing reduces.
  
  WHAT HIGH ESSENTIAL COMPLEXITY SIGNALS:
    Irreducible CFG subgraphs that survive reduction are the "tangled" portions.
    These are program constructs (typically gotos, break-to-outer-loop,
    multiple-exit loops) that CANNOT be replaced by structured equivalents
    without adding boolean flags or duplicating code.
    Essential complexity = 1 is the refactoring target.
  
  SIMPLIFICATION IMPLICATION FOR pflow:
    ev(G) - 1 = number of unstructured "excess" control flows to eliminate.
    Each reduction of ev(G) by 1 = one unstructured jump successfully structured.
  
  RELATIONSHIP TO V(G):
    1 <= ev(G) <= V(G)
    V(G) - ev(G) = complexity attributable to structured control (removable by
                   decomposition into subroutines without loss of structure)
    ev(G) captures the "residual" non-structurable complexity.
  
  PRIME DECOMPOSITION / INTERVAL ANALYSIS:
    McCabe uses Cocke's interval analysis: decompose CFG into intervals
    (maximal single-entry subgraphs). Iteratively replace each interval
    with a single node to obtain the "derived sequence" of graphs.
    If the derived sequence reduces to a single node: structured (ev=1).
    If the process stalls (irreducible graph remains): ev(G) = V(G_irreducible).
    T1: Remove self-loops; T2: Merge node with single predecessor into predecessor
    (collapses sequential chains and structured constructs).
    Primes = subgraphs that survive all possible T1-T2 reductions.
    Each prime with k exits contributes (k-1) to essential complexity.
  
  EXAMPLE:
    for-loop with inner if-statement: ev(G) = 1 (fully reducible, structured).
    Loop with goto jumping outside the loop: ev(G) >= 2 (irreducible subgraph).
    Program using goto to implement "find first zero row": ev(G) = 4 (example from wiki).
  
  ADA ESSENTIAL COMPLEXITY VARIANT:
    Count V(G) only for code remaining after excluding pure-structural Ada
    control statements. A compound statement is non-structural if it contains
    a goto transferring control outside the operator.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 3
ts: 2026-05-29T00:00:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Cognitive Complexity: A new way of measuring understandability
authors: G. Ann Campbell
year: 2018
venue: SonarSource White Paper (ACM TechDebt 2018 conference paper companion)
quality: INDUSTRY
url: https://www.sonarsource.com/resources/cognitive-complexity/
keywords: cognitive complexity, nesting increment, structural increment, hybrid increment, fundamental increment, boolean sequence, understandability, sonar
cites: McCabe 1976
cited_by: SonarQube S3776, CodeClimate, numerous IDE plugins
content:
  MOTIVATION: Cyclomatic complexity measures testability (path count) but
    does not track human-perceived difficulty of understanding code.
    Cognitive Complexity breaks from mathematical models to assess maintainability.
    Key claim: a && b && c && d is not 4x harder than a && b; CC treats them equal.
  
  FOUR TYPES OF INCREMENTS:
  
  1. NESTING INCREMENT: Added for each level of nesting at the point of assessment.
     Applied whenever a control flow structure is nested inside another.
     Amount = current nesting depth at that point.
     Example: if inside while inside if = nesting depth 3 at innermost structure.
  
  2. STRUCTURAL INCREMENT (+1 + nesting penalty):
     Structures that both take the increment AND increase nesting count for children:
       - for, while, do-while (loop structures)
       - if, #if, #ifdef (conditional structures)
       - ternary operator ? :
     Each gets +1 structural increment PLUS nesting penalty based on current depth.
     These structures increase the nesting counter for any structures inside them.
  
  3. HYBRID INCREMENT (+0 at their level but increase nesting for children):
     Structures that do NOT get their own nesting penalty but still increase the
     nesting count:
       - else if, elif, else (no extra nesting increment for the else/elif itself,
         since "the mental cost has already been paid when reading the if")
       - catch, except (in some language mappings)
     These still increase nesting depth for code inside them.
  
  4. FUNDAMENTAL INCREMENT (+1, no nesting penalty, do not increase nesting):
     Structures that always add 1 regardless of nesting:
       - break with label / goto label (jumps to non-obvious targets)
       - sequences of binary logical operators: each SEQUENCE (not each operator)
         adds +1. "a && b && c && d" = 1 increment (one sequence of &&).
         "a || b && c || d" = 2 increments (two distinct operator types in sequence).
       - recursion: a method calling itself directly or indirectly.
  
  EXCLUDED (NO INCREMENT):
     - try and finally blocks
     - null-coalescing operators (?? / ?: / Elvis operator)
     - method declarations / function definitions themselves
     - shorthand expressions that replace multi-line code
  
  SWITCH STATEMENT RULE:
     switch/match/when combined = 1 structural increment for the whole switch.
     Individual case branches do NOT each get an increment.
     (This differs from cyclomatic complexity which counts each case.)
  
  NESTING RULES DETAIL:
     Nesting counter increments at: if, for, while, do-while, ternary, switch,
     try (in some implementations), lambda/closure/nested function definitions.
     Counter resets at end of each structure.
     else/elif: does NOT add to nesting count for siblings; DOES increase depth
     for code nested inside the else block.
  
  EXAMPLE SCORES (from white paper):
     sumOfPrimes (double nested loop + labeled break): cognitive = 4
     getWords (switch + nested conditions): cyclomatic = 4, cognitive = 4
     (illustrating cases where CC and Cognitive match or diverge)
  
  BOOLEAN SEQUENCE RULE DETAIL:
     For "a && b || c && d": two sequences (&&-sequence, &&-sequence after ||) = +2
     For "a && b && c": one sequence = +1
     Mixed operators that do NOT form separate sequences collapse to fewer increments.
  
  COMPARISON TO CYCLOMATIC:
     Cyclomatic: each binary logical operator in a condition = +1 (counts paths).
     Cognitive: a sequence of same-type operators = +1 (counts cognitive groups).
     Cyclomatic counts switch cases individually; Cognitive counts switch once.
     Cyclomatic penalizes short-circuit expressions the same as nested loops;
     Cognitive penalizes nesting more heavily than non-nested branches.
  
  DEFAULT SONAR THRESHOLD: 15 (rule S3776 across Java, C#, C++, Python, TypeScript)
  SONAR RULE ID: S3776 ("Cognitive Complexity of functions should not be too high")
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 4
ts: 2026-05-29T00:00:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: NPATH: A Measure of Execution Path Complexity and Its Applications
authors: Brian A. Nejmeh
year: 1988
venue: Communications of the ACM, Vol. 31, No. 2, pp. 188-200 (Feb 1988)
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/NPATH:-a-measure-of-execution-path-complexity-and-Nejmeh/887c306420cbe4332526af7e62380e963751bfe4
keywords: NPATH, execution path, acyclic paths, branch testing, test case generation
cites: McCabe 1976
cited_by: Checkstyle NPathComplexityCheck, PMD, numerous static analyzers
content:
  DEFINITION: NPATH = number of acyclic execution paths through a function/method.
    "Acyclic" = no path traverses the same sequence of instructions twice.
    Differs from McCabe: NPATH is multiplicative (combinatorial explosion),
    McCabe is additive. NPATH grows exponentially with nested conditionals.
  
  COMPUTATION FORMULA BY STATEMENT TYPE:
    (NP(X) = NPATH complexity of statement/block X)
  
    if statement (no else):
      NP(if) = NP(if-range) + 1 + NP(expr)
      (path through body + path skipping body + boolean paths in condition)
  
    if-else statement:
      NP(if-else) = NP(if-range) + NP(else-range) + NP(expr)
      (paths through then-branch + paths through else-branch + boolean paths)
  
    while loop:
      NP(while) = NP(while-range) + NP(expr) + 1
      (paths through body + boolean paths + path skipping loop entirely)
  
    do-while loop:
      NP(do-while) = NP(do-range) + NP(expr) + 1
  
    for loop:
      NP(for) = NP(for-range) + NP(expr1) + NP(expr2) + NP(expr3) + 1
      (body paths + 3 expression boolean paths + skip-loop path)
  
    switch statement:
      NP(switch) = sum(NP(case-range[i])) + NP(default-range) + NP(expr)
  
    ternary operator (expr1 ? expr2 : expr3):
      NP(ternary) = NP(expr1) + NP(expr2) + NP(expr3) + 2
  
    Boolean expressions (within conditions):
      NP(expr) = count of && and || operators (0 if no boolean operators)
  
    goto, break, continue, return:
      NP = 1 each
  
    Function/method declaration:
      NP(function) = product of NP(statement[i]) over all statements... 
      Actually: NP(function) = sum of NP(statement[i]) for sequential statements
      (sequential statements multiply paths; parallel branches add)
  
    Single statement / empty block / function call:
      NP = 1
  
  KEY PROPERTY: For nested conditionals, NPATH is multiplicative.
    Example: two nested if-else blocks = 2 * 2 = 4 (not 2+2=4 as in cyclomatic).
    Three nested if-else blocks = 2 * 2 * 2 = 8 (vs 3+1=4 cyclomatic).
    This captures the combinatorial explosion of test cases needed.
  
  THRESHOLD: Informal NPATH limit of 200 at AT&T Bell Labs.
    Functions > 200 are candidates for decomposition.
    Checkstyle default threshold: 200.
  
  RELATIONSHIP TO CYCLOMATIC:
    Both measure control flow complexity but NPATH is more sensitive to nesting.
    NPATH "designed to avoid problems like nesting level sensitivity" of cyclomatic.
    NPATH explicitly counts boolean paths within conditions via NP(expr).
  
  PRACTICAL NOTE: NPATH can grow astronomically; even modest functions with
    deeply nested conditions can exceed thousands. This is a signal of
    untestability, not just complexity.
<<<END>>>


SOURCE
id: 5
ts: 2026-05-29T00:01:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Control Flow, Data Flow and Program Complexity
authors: Enrique I. Oviedo
year: 1980
venue: Proceedings IEEE COMPSAC, Chicago, pp. 146-152
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://dl.acm.org/doi/abs/10.5555/911473
keywords: data flow complexity, def-use pairs, control flow complexity, program complexity, weighted sum
cites: McCabe 1976, Halstead 1977
cited_by: Beck/Beyer DepDegree, Weyuker evaluations of complexity metrics, Theoretical Approaches in Software Complexity Metrics
content:
  MOTIVATION: Review of published control-flow complexity measures reveals 
    three deficiencies: (1) loss of information, (2) lack of specificity,
    (3) lack of analytical support.
  
  OVIEDO'S COMPOSITE METRIC:
    C(P) = w1 * CF(P) + w2 * DF(P)
    where CF(P) = control flow complexity (e.g., V(G) or similar)
          DF(P) = data flow complexity
          w1, w2 = weighting constants (relative importance)
  
  DATA FLOW COMPLEXITY DF(P):
    DF(P) = number of def-use associations (pairs) in the program P.
    A def-use association (d, u) exists where d is a definition of variable x
    and u is a subsequent use of x reachable from d without an intervening
    definition of x on that path.
    DF(P) = sum over all variables x of |{(d,u) : d defines x, u uses x,
            path d->u with no intervening def of x}|
  
  EQUIVALENTLY: DF(P) = total number of reaching def-use pairs across all
    variables and all definition and use points.
  
  INTERPRETATION: Higher DF means more data connections to track while reading
    the program. Data flow complexity captures the "state holding" burden on
    the reader that CF alone does not capture.
  
  NOTE: The exact formula is reconstructed from citations; original paper is
    behind ACM paywall. Later works (e.g. Weyuker 1988, Beyer 2010) confirm
    this formulation.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 6
ts: 2026-05-29T00:02:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: A Simple and Effective Measure for Complex Low-Level Dependencies (DepDegree)
authors: Dirk Beyer, Armaghan Fararooy
year: 2010
venue: Proceedings of ICPC (International Conference on Program Comprehension), pp. 80-83, IEEE
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://www.sosy-lab.org/research/DepDegreeProperties/
keywords: DepDegree, data flow dependency, use-def graph, program comprehension, Weyuker properties
cites: Oviedo 1980, Rapps-Weyuker 1985, Miller's 7+/-2 (psychological basis)
cited_by: Beyer-Haring 2014 (Weyuker properties), Peitek 2021 fMRI study
content:
  DEFINITION:
    Use-def graph S_G = (B, E) where:
      B = set of program operations (statements/basic blocks)
      E = set of dependency edges: (b_u, b_d) in E iff variable x is used in b_u
          and b_d is a reaching definition of x at b_u (no redefinition between)
    
    DepDegree of operation b:
      dd_G(b) = |{b' in B : (b, b') in E}|
      = number of operations that provided definitions reaching b
    
    DepDegree of program (CFG G):
      dd(G) = sum_{b in B} dd_G(b) = |E|
      = total number of edges in the data-flow (use-def) graph
  
  PSYCHOLOGICAL BASIS:
    Miller's Law: short-term memory limited to 7 +/- 2 chunks.
    Programs where many statements depend on many prior definitions exceed
    working-memory capacity. DepDegree quantifies the total dependency load.
  
  COMPUTATION FROM DEF-USE:
    For each statement s that uses variable x:
      rd_G(s, x) = set of statements that define x and reach s
                 (standard reaching definitions computation from CFG)
      dd_G(s) += |rd_G(s, x)| for each x used in s
    Sum over all s: dd(G)
  
  WEYUKER PROPERTIES:
    Beyer & Haring (2014, ICPC) formally proved DepDegree satisfies ALL
    of Weyuker's 9 properties for complexity metrics (unlike V(G) which
    fails some). This is a significant theoretical result.
  
  SIMPLIFICATION IMPLICATION:
    High DepDegree statements = statements that depend on many prior definitions.
    Reducing DepDegree by splitting a function or reducing variable scope
    directly reduces the "memory load" a reader must hold.
    Per-statement dd_G(b) pinpoints which exact statements are the dependency
    hotspots -- these are the refactoring targets.
  
  NOTE ON NAMING: The user prompt attributes this to "Beck & Diehl 2011."
    Actual authors are Beyer & Fararooy 2010 (ICPC). Beck/Diehl are separate
    ICPC 2013 researchers. Possible confusion in attribution.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 7
ts: 2026-05-29T00:03:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: A Formal Evaluation of DepDegree Based on Weyuker's Properties
authors: Dirk Beyer, Max Haring
year: 2014
venue: Proceedings of 22nd ICPC (International Conference on Program Comprehension)
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://dl.acm.org/doi/abs/10.1145/2597008.2597794
keywords: DepDegree, Weyuker properties, data flow complexity, formal evaluation, software metrics
cites: Beyer-Fararooy 2010, Weyuker 1988, Oviedo 1980
cited_by: Enhancing Software Defect Prediction (Springer 2024)
content:
  PURPOSE: Formally verify DepDegree satisfies all 9 Weyuker properties,
    establishing it as a theoretically sound complexity metric.
  
  WEYUKER'S NINE PROPERTIES (briefly):
    1. Non-trivial: not all programs have the same complexity
    2. Programs with different complexity exist
    3. Two equivalent programs may differ in complexity
    4. Renaming doesn't change complexity
    5. Adding code doesn't decrease complexity (monotonicity)
    6. Complexity of composite > max of parts
    7. Order of statements matters
    8. Nesting matters
    9. Different programs can have same complexity
  
  RESULT: DepDegree satisfies all 9. McCabe's V(G) fails property 3 (two
    programs with same control flow structure have same V(G) regardless of
    data).
  
  SIGNIFICANCE FOR pflow: DepDegree is the only commonly used data-flow
    complexity metric with a complete formal justification.
  
  REACHING DEFINITIONS:
    rd_G: B x Var -> P(B)  (program operation, variable) -> set of definitions
    Formally: rd_G(b, x) = {b' in B : b' assigns x, there exists path b'->b
              in CFG with no intermediate assignment to x}
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 8
ts: 2026-05-29T00:04:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Variable Span / Live Range as Complexity Metric
authors: (attributed to Elshoff 1976, Oviedo 1980, Harrison & Magel 1981)
year: 1976
venue: Various; concept synthesized across multiple papers
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/dsa/complexity-metrics/
keywords: variable span, live range, average span, def-use distance, state holding, program comprehension
cites: Elshoff 1976, Harrison-Magel 1981
cited_by: Numerous complexity surveys, program comprehension studies
content:
  DEFINITION - SPAN:
    For a variable x referenced at N distinct points in a module,
    there are (N-1) spans for x.
    Span_i(x) = number of executable statements between successive references i
                and i+1 of x.
    Average span of x: avg_span(x) = (1/(N-1)) * sum_{i=1}^{N-1} Span_i(x)
    
    Module-level metric: average over all variables of their average span.
  
  DEFINITION - LIVE RANGE:
    Live range of variable x at statement s: x is "live" at s if there exists
    a path from s to a use of x without an intervening definition of x.
    Live range = set of statements where x is live = [def, last-use] interval.
    
    Live range length for x across definition d to last use u:
      LR(x,d,u) = number of statements between d and u (inclusive).
    
    Total live range complexity: sum over all definitions d of all variables
      of LR(x,d,last_use_of_x_from_d).
  
  INTERPRETATION:
    Large span = reader must remember a variable's value across many intervening
    statements. "The larger the span, the more complex the module."
    Live range overlap = number of simultaneously live variables = register
    pressure = cognitive load of tracking live state simultaneously.
  
  SIMPLIFICATION IMPLICATION FOR pflow:
    Long live ranges flag variables whose scope should be narrowed.
    High span between def and last use = opportunity to move definition closer
    to its use, or split the function.
    Simultaneously live variables (live range interference graph density) =
    "how much state must the reader hold at once."
  
  LIVE-RANGE COMPLEXITY FORMULA (Harrison/Oviedo variant):
    LV(P) = sum over all variables x of |live_range(x)|
             where |live_range(x)| = number of statements in the live range of x
    Normalized: LV_avg = LV(P) / (num_statements * num_variables)
  
  NOTE: The exact original attribution is unclear; several 1976-1982 papers
    define variants. Span = statements between successive uses; live range =
    statements between def and last use. These are related but distinct.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 9
ts: 2026-05-29T00:05:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: A Measure of Control Flow Complexity in Program Text (Knots)
authors: M.R. Woodward, M.A. Hennell, D. Hedley
year: 1979
venue: IEEE Transactions on Software Engineering, Vol. SE-5, No. 1, pp. 45-50
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/1702586/
keywords: knots, control flow complexity, edge crossings, unstructured code, linearized program, program text
cites:
cited_by: Various software complexity surveys
content:
  DEFINITION - KNOTS:
    A "knot" is a pair of control flow jumps (arcs in the linearized program text)
    that cross each other when the program is laid out linearly (textually).
    
    Formally: Given the linear ordering of statements s_1, s_2, ..., s_n,
    a pair of jumps (s_i -> s_j) and (s_k -> s_l) forms a knot if:
      i < k < j < l  OR  k < i < l < j
      (the intervals [i,j] and [k,l] overlap but neither contains the other)
    
    Knot count = number of such crossing pairs in the linearized text.
  
  INTERPRETATION:
    Knots = 0: Program is fully structured (or at least planar in linear layout).
    Knots > 0: Program has interleaved/crossed control transfers = unstructured.
    Each knot = one "tangling" where two control paths cross in textual order.
  
  RELATIONSHIP TO ESSENTIAL COMPLEXITY:
    Knots measure unstructuredness independently of McCabe's reduction method.
    Both target the same phenomenon (unstructured/goto code) from different angles.
    Knots are purely textual; essential complexity is graph-theoretic.
    A program with many gotos will have high knot count AND high essential complexity.
  
  DISTINCTION FROM CYCLOMATIC:
    Cyclomatic V(G) = E - N + 2: measures branching density, not structure.
    A structured program with many if-else branches has high V(G) but 0 knots.
    Knots specifically measure non-structured transfers, not branching per se.
  
  COMPUTATION:
    1. Linearize the program text (label statements 1..n).
    2. Extract all control flow arcs (source, target) where source != target-1
       (i.e., non-fall-through arcs: gotos, conditional jumps, loop back-edges).
    3. Count pairs of arcs (i->j, k->l) where i<k<j<l (standard interval overlap).
    Time complexity: O(m^2) where m = number of non-fall-through arcs.
  
  NOTE: Authors are Woodward, Hennell, Hedley (not Duran — Duran is a different
    author associated with structural testing). Confirmed by IEEE Xplore record
    and multiple citation records.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 10
ts: 2026-05-29T00:06:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Halstead Complexity Measures (Elements of Software Science)
authors: Maurice H. Halstead
year: 1977
venue: Elsevier North-Holland (book), "Elements of Software Science"
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halstead_complexity_measures
keywords: Halstead, volume, difficulty, effort, operators, operands, vocabulary, software science
cites:
cited_by: McCabe 1976 comparisons, numerous surveys, Verifysoft, SciTools
content:
  BASE COUNTS:
    n1 = number of distinct operators
    n2 = number of distinct operands
    N1 = total occurrences of operators
    N2 = total occurrences of operands
  
  DERIVED METRICS:
    Program Length:    N = N1 + N2
    Vocabulary Size:   n = n1 + n2
    Estimated Length:  N_hat = n1*log2(n1) + n2*log2(n2)
    
    Volume:     V = N * log2(n)
      Represents information content (mathematical bits).
      Recommended range: 20-1000 per function; 100-8000 per file.
    
    Difficulty: D = (n1/2) * (N2/n2)
      Measures error-proneness; proportional to unique operators
      and operand reuse rate.
    
    Level:      L = 1/D
      Inverse of difficulty; lower L = higher error risk.
    
    Effort:     E = V * D = V/L
      Cognitive effort to implement or understand; unit = elementary
      mental discriminations.
    
    Time:       T = E / 18   (seconds)
      Empirical constant 18 = Stroud number (elementary mental 
      discriminations per second for human cognition).
    
    Delivered Bugs: B = E^(2/3) / 3000
      Alternate formula: B = V / 3000
      Files should contain < 2 estimated bugs.
    
    Language Level: L_hat = V / (D * V_hat)
      Higher = higher-level language; more concise expression.
  
  OPERATORS vs OPERANDS (definition):
    Operators: reserved words (if, while, for, +, *, =, etc.), punctuation (;, ,)
    Operands: variable names, literal constants, labels
    Note: Definitions vary by language and tool implementation.
  
  LIMITATION FOR pflow:
    Halstead metrics are lexical, not structural. They do not use CFG, def-use,
    or dominators. They capture vocabulary/size complexity, not flow complexity.
    Included here for completeness; less actionable for flow-based refactoring.
<<<END>>>


SOURCE
id: 11
ts: 2026-05-29T00:07:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Cognitive Complexity Scoring Algorithm (precise specification)
authors: G. Ann Campbell / SonarSource; algorithm reconstruction from multiple sources
year: 2018
venue: SonarSource, various implementations (sonar-java, complexipy, etc.)
quality: INDUSTRY
url: https://blog.scitools.com/cognitive-complexity-metric-plugin/
keywords: cognitive complexity, nesting increment, structural increment, exact algorithm, sumOfPrimes example
cites: Campbell 2018 white paper
cited_by: SonarQube, complexipy, flake8-cognitive-complexity, many IDE plugins
content:
  ALGORITHM (reconstructed from multiple sources):
  
  STATE: nesting_depth = 0 (tracks current structural nesting level)
  
  FOR EACH CONSTRUCT in program text:
  
  STRUCTURAL INCREMENTS (add 1 + nesting_depth; THEN increment nesting_depth):
    - if
    - ternary operator (? :)
    - for / foreach
    - while
    - do-while
    - switch (entire block = 1 increment, NOT per case)
    - catch / except / rescue
    INCREMENT = 1 + nesting_depth
    AFTER: nesting_depth += 1 (for children inside this construct)
    AT END OF CONSTRUCT: nesting_depth -= 1
  
  HYBRID INCREMENTS (add 1; THEN increment nesting_depth; but NO nesting penalty):
    - else if / elif / elsif
    - else
    INCREMENT = 1 (no + nesting_depth)
    AFTER: nesting_depth += 1 (code inside else/elif still deeper)
    AT END: nesting_depth -= 1
  
  FUNDAMENTAL INCREMENTS (add 1; DO NOT change nesting_depth):
    - goto <label>  (unconditional jump to non-obvious target)
    - break <label> / continue <label>  (labeled jump)
    - recursion (direct or mutual; one +1 per recursive call)
    - binary logical operator SEQUENCE:
        Each SEQUENCE of SAME-TYPE operators = +1
        e.g.: "a && b && c" = 1 (one && sequence)
              "a || b && c" = 2 (one || sequence, one && sequence)
              "a && b || c && d" = 2 (same)
        NOTE: Each contiguous run of same logical operator type = 1 increment.
              Switching operator type starts a new sequence (+1 more).
  
  NO INCREMENT (zero contribution):
    - try / finally / else-on-try blocks
    - null-coalescing (??, ?: Elvis operator)
    - method/function declaration
    - class declaration
    - standard method calls
    - comprehensions (in most implementations)
    - import statements
  
  WORKED EXAMPLE: sumOfPrimes(int max)
    Code:
      int total = 0;
      OUT: for (int i = 1; i <= max; ++i) {   // FOR: +1+0=+1, nesting->1
        for (int j = 2; j < i; ++j) {          // FOR: +1+1=+2, nesting->2
          if (i % j == 0)                      // IF: +1+2=+3, nesting->3
            continue OUT;                      // labeled continue: +1
        }                                       // nesting->2
      }                                         // nesting->1 after inner for; ->0 after outer
      return total;
    TOTAL: 1 + 2 + 3 + 1 = 7
  
  WORKED EXAMPLE: getWords(int number)
    Code:
      switch (number) {              // SWITCH: +1+0=+1, nesting->1
        case 1: return "one";        // no increment (case in switch)
        case 2: return "a couple";   // no increment
        default: return "lots";      // no increment
      }                              // nesting->0
    TOTAL: 1
    Cyclomatic complexity: 4 (counts each case separately)
    
  COMPARISON TABLE: sumOfPrimes
    Cyclomatic = 4 (2 for-loops + 1 if = 3 decisions + 1)
    Cognitive  = 7 (nesting penalties reveal deeper complexity)
  
  SONAR RULE: S3776, default threshold = 15 (configurable)
  LANGUAGES: Java, C#, C++, Python, TypeScript, JavaScript, Go, Ruby, etc.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 12
ts: 2026-05-29T00:08:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Nesting Depth / Maximum Nesting Level as Complexity Metric
authors: Multiple (Harrison & Magel 1981; Nesting as independent metric in tools)
year: 1981
venue: ACM SIGPLAN Notices, Vol. 16, No. 3, pp. 63-74 (Harrison & Magel original)
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/947825.947829
keywords: nesting depth, nesting level, conditional nesting, loop nesting, DCOND, DLOOP, indentation depth
cites: McCabe 1976
cited_by: ABAP code-pal, SAP tools, ESLint max-depth, CodeScene nesting_depth_warning
content:
  DEFINITION:
    Nesting depth at statement s = number of currently open control structures
    that contain s. Corresponds to indentation level in well-formatted code.
    
    Max nesting depth of function = max over all statements of nesting depth.
    
  HARRISON-MAGEL 1981 METRIC:
    Weighted nesting complexity = sum over all statements s of (depth(s))
    where depth(s) = nesting level of s (1-indexed from function body).
    (Weights deeper statements more heavily to penalize deep nesting.)
  
  VARIANT METRICS:
    DCOND = max depth of conditional (if/switch) nesting across all branches.
           Recommended maximum: 5 (various style guides).
    DLOOP = max depth of loop nesting.
           Recommended maximum: 2 loops per procedure.
    
  THRESHOLDS IN TOOLS:
    ESLint max-depth rule: configurable, default not fixed; commonly 4.
    SAP ABAP code-pal: threshold = 5 (max nesting depth within modularization unit).
    CodeScene function_nesting_depth_warning: configurable, example default = 3.
    Cognitive Complexity: nesting depth is built-in penalty (not a separate metric).
    MathWorks Polyspace: "Depth of call nesting exceeds threshold" inspector.
  
  SIMPLIFICATION IMPLICATION FOR pflow:
    Each level of nesting that can be removed (early return, extract method,
    invert condition) reduces max_nesting_depth and all weighted nesting metrics.
    "Guard clause" pattern: replace if (cond) { body } with if (!cond) return;
    reduces nesting by 1 for entire body.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 13
ts: 2026-05-29T00:09:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Program Dependence Graph and Its Use in Optimization (PDG)
authors: Jeanne Ferrante, Karl J. Ottenstein, Joe D. Warren
year: 1987
venue: ACM Transactions on Programming Languages and Systems (TOPLAS), Vol. 9, pp. 319-349
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/24039.24041
keywords: program dependence graph, PDG, control dependence, data dependence, post-dominator, CFG, slicing
cites: Ottenstein & Ottenstein 1984 (PDG in dev environment)
cited_by: DepDegree, program slicing, virtually all PDG-based analysis work
content:
  PROGRAM DEPENDENCE GRAPH (PDG) DEFINITION:
    Directed graph G_P = (V, E) where:
      V = vertices = one per statement/predicate + region vertices (ENTRY, etc.)
      E = edges of two types:
        (1) Data dependence edges: (s, t) if s defines variable x, t uses x,
            and there exists a def-use path s->t in CFG with no intervening def of x.
        (2) Control dependence edges: (p, s) if predicate p controls whether s executes.
    
    Combined: edges make explicit both data flow and control flow for each operation.
  
  CONTROL DEPENDENCE CONSTRUCTION:
    Statement y is control-dependent on predicate x iff:
      (a) there exists a CFG path from x to y on which y post-dominates every node
          after x on the path, AND
      (b) x does not post-dominate y.
    
    Equivalently: y is in the post-dominator frontier of x in the reverse CFG.
    
    Algorithm:
      1. Compute post-dominator tree of CFG (using Lengauer-Tarjan, O(N log N)).
      2. For each edge (x, y) in CFG where y does NOT post-dominate x:
         Walk up post-dominator tree from y to (but not including) the LCA of x
         in the post-dominator tree; all nodes on this walk are control-dependent on x.
  
  DATA DEPENDENCE CONSTRUCTION:
    Standard reaching definitions / def-use chains.
    (s, t) is a data dependence edge iff s defines x, t uses x,
    and s is in reaching_defs(t, x).
  
  RELEVANCE FOR pflow:
    Control dependence graph CDs = post-dominator frontier.
    A statement with many control dependents = a branching point controlling
    many other statements = candidate for simplification (reduce scope of predicate).
    Data dependence slice at statement s = all statements that must execute before s
    to produce correct value = the "closure" of s under backward data deps.
    Slice size = complexity of the "what must be tracked" metric.
  
  METRICS DERIVABLE FROM PDG:
    - Fan-in of data deps per statement = DepDegree of that statement.
    - Control dependence height = depth in CDS = nesting complexity.
    - PDG "width" at a control node = number of statements it controls = scope of a branch.
    - Dense PDG = high coupling between statements = hard to simplify without side effects.
<<<END>>>

