id: 14
ts: 2026-05-29T00:10:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: SonarQube Complexity Rules: S3776 (Cognitive) and S1541 (Cyclomatic)
authors: SonarSource
year: 2018
venue: SonarQube Rule Documentation
quality: INDUSTRY
url: https://next.sonarqube.com/sonarqube/coding_rules?open=java%3AS3776&rule_key=java%3AS3776
keywords: SonarQube, S3776, S1541, cognitive complexity, cyclomatic complexity, code smell, threshold 15
cites: Campbell 2018
cited_by: Numerous SonarQube deployments
content:
  RULE S3776 - Cognitive Complexity of functions should not be too high:
    Type: Code Smell (Maintainability)
    Default threshold: 15
    Languages: Java (java:S3776), C# (csharpsquid:S3776), C++ (cpp:S3776),
               Python (python:S3776), TypeScript (typescript:S3776), etc.
    What triggers: cognitive complexity of any function > threshold
    Increments: loop structures, conditionals, catches, switches, jumps to labels,
                conditions mixing multiple operators, each nesting level.
    Configurable: Yes, threshold adjustable in Quality Profile.
    
  RULE S1541 - Cyclomatic Complexity of functions should not be too high:
    Type: Code Smell
    Default threshold: varies by language (10 in some, 20 in others)
    Languages: Java, C, C++, others
    What triggers: V(G) > threshold for a function
    Note: S3776 is PREFERRED over S1541 by SonarSource for maintainability assessment.
  
  CYCLOMATIC COMPLEXITY THRESHOLDS USED BY SONAR:
    Rule fires at V(G) > 20 (Quality Gate warning) in some configurations.
    Individual function: V(G) > 10 is commonly the code-smell threshold.
  
  REFACTORING GUIDANCE:
    SonarQube suggests splitting functions, using early returns (guard clauses),
    extracting complex conditions into named boolean variables or methods.
  
  EFFORT-TO-FIX: Not specified in S3776 by default.
    GitHub issue requesting effort formula was open as of ~2018.
<<<END>>>

