SOURCE
id: 18
ts: 2026-05-29T00:14:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Radon: Python Code Metrics Tool (CC, Halstead, Maintainability Index)
authors: Michele Lacchia (rubik)
year: 2014
venue: PyPI / GitHub: rubik/radon
quality: CODE
url: https://radon.readthedocs.io/en/latest/intro.html
keywords: radon, cyclomatic complexity, Halstead, maintainability index, Python, AST visitor
cites: McCabe 1976, Halstead 1977, SEI Maintainability Index formula
cited_by: Various Python complexity analysis pipelines
content:
  CYCLOMATIC COMPLEXITY (radon implementation):
    Formula: V(G) = decisions + 1
    Counted structures (each +1 unless noted):
      if, elif (+1 each), else (+0), for (+1), while (+1),
      except (+1), finally (+0), with (+1), assert (+1),
      comprehensions (+1), boolean operators: and/or (+1 each)
    Implementation: AST visitor (ComplexityVisitor class)
    File: radon/visitors.py, radon/complexity.py
    
  RANK SCALE FOR CYCLOMATIC COMPLEXITY:
    A: 1-5   (low risk — simple block)
    B: 6-10  (low risk — well structured and stable block)
    C: 11-15 (moderate risk — slightly complex block)
    D: 16-20 (more than moderate risk — more complex block)
    E: 21-25 (high risk — complex block, alarming)
    F: 26+   (very high risk — error-prone, unstable block)
  
  HALSTEAD METRICS (radon/metrics.py, exact code):
    h = h1 + h2           # vocabulary (η = η1 + η2)
    N = N1 + N2           # length
    length_hat = h1*log2(h1) + h2*log2(h2)  # estimated length
    volume = N * log2(h)  # V
    difficulty = (h1 * N2) / (2.0 * h2)  # D
    effort = difficulty * volume           # E
    time = effort / 18.0                   # T (seconds)
    bugs = volume / 3000.0                 # B
  
  MAINTAINABILITY INDEX (radon/metrics.py, exact code):
    MI = max(0, (171 - 5.2*ln(V) - 0.23*G - 16.2*ln(L) + 50*sin(sqrt(2.46 * radians(C)))) * 100 / 171)
    V = Halstead volume
    G = total cyclomatic complexity
    L = SLOC (source lines of code)
    C = percent comment lines (as percentage 0-100)
    RANK: A (score > 19), B (9 < score <= 19), C (score <= 9)
    NOTE: "Very experimental metric." MI from original SEI paper (Coleman et al. 1994).
<<<END>>>

