id: 19
ts: 2026-05-29T00:15:00Z
relevance: MED
title: Structured Testing: A Software Testing Methodology Using the Cyclomatic Complexity Metric
authors: Arthur H. Watson, T.J. McCabe (NIST)
year: 1996
venue: NIST Special Publication 500-235
quality: INDUSTRY
url: https://www.mccabe.com/pdf/mccabe-nist235r.pdf
keywords: structured testing, cyclomatic complexity, basis path testing, NIST, McCabe, threshold, essential complexity
cites: McCabe 1976
cited_by: FAA, NASA, MISRA, numerous safety-critical standards
content:
  THRESHOLDS (from NIST 500-235):
    10-15: Typical range for significant majority of functions.
    V(G) > 10: Functions warrant scrutiny and simplification.
    
  MISRA RECOMMENDATION: Maximum V(G) = 15 for safety-critical software.
  FAA: Recommends using cyclomatic complexity for measuring and reducing complexity.
  NASA: Recommends cyclomatic complexity metrics for modular design.
  
  BAD FIX PROBABILITY (from RIAC/DoD data cited in various sources):
    V(G) 1-10:   5% bad fix probability (per Walsh 1979 embedded systems data)
    V(G) 20-30:  20% bad fix probability
    V(G) > 50:   Cannot be tested
    V(G) > 75:   "Every change may trigger a bad fix"
  
  STRUCTURED TESTING METHODOLOGY:
    Uses V(G) to determine minimum test cases: need V(G) tests to achieve
    full branch coverage. Essential complexity ev(G) indicates whether
    the test cases can be reduced by refactoring unstructured code.
<<<END>>>

