id: 20
ts: 2026-05-29T00:16:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Lizard: Extensible Cyclomatic Complexity Analyzer
authors: Terry Yin (terryyin)
year: 2019
venue: GitHub: terryyin/lizard; PyPI; ASCL 1906.011
quality: CODE
url: https://github.com/terryyin/lizard
keywords: Lizard, cyclomatic complexity, cognitive complexity, multi-language, token count, NLOC
cites: McCabe 1976
cited_by: Codacy, various CI pipelines
content:
  METRICS COMPUTED:
    - NLOC: Non-comment lines of code per function
    - CCN: Cyclomatic Complexity Number
    - Token count per function
    - Parameter count per function
  
  CCN COMPUTATION:
    "Mostly compatible with McCabe's theory" with exception:
    switch/case: counted by NUMBER OF CASES, not by switch block.
    Each case branch = +1 (unlike SonarSource cognitive complexity which
    counts the switch block as 1).
    Boolean operators: and, or each +1.
    
  DEFAULT THRESHOLD: 15 (configurable via -C flag)
    Functions exceeding threshold generate warnings.
    Exit code non-zero if any warnings.
  
  COGNITIVE COMPLEXITY:
    Supported as an extension.
    Suppression: #lizard forgives comment in source.
  
  LANGUAGES: Java, C/C++, C#, JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, Ruby, Swift,
             Scala, GDScript, Go, PHP, Rust, Lua, Kotlin, and more.
  
  NESTED STRUCTURE EXTENSION: -ENS flag counts nested control structures separately.
<<<END>>>


SOURCE
id: 21
ts: 2026-05-29T00:17:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: gnatmetric Ada Essential Complexity (Modified McCabe for Ada)
authors: AdaCore / GCC project
year: 2013
venue: GNAT User's Guide, GCC 4.8+; GNAT SAS gnatmetric documentation
quality: DOCS
url: https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc-4.8.4/gnat_ugn/Complexity-Metrics-Control.html
keywords: gnatmetric, essential complexity, Ada, non-structural, goto, raise, return, cyclomatic
cites: McCabe 1976
cited_by:
content:
  GNAT METRICS COMPUTED:
    1. McCabe cyclomatic complexity (statement + expression complexity)
    2. McCabe essential complexity (Ada-modified)
    3. Maximal loop nesting level
    4. Extra exit points (return/raise counts)
  
  CYCLOMATIC COMPLEXITY:
    Three values computed per subprogram body:
      (a) Statement complexity: control statements only
          (if, loop, case, select, exception handlers)
      (b) Expression complexity: short-circuit forms only
          (and then, or else, and also Ada 2012 quantified/conditional exprs)
      (c) Total = (a) + (b)
    Option: --no-static-loop excludes static loops from count.
  
  ADA ESSENTIAL COMPLEXITY (modified McCabe):
    Removes "pure structural" control statements from graph, then computes V(G).
    NON-STRUCTURAL (NOT removed; contribute to essential complexity):
      - raise statement as subcomponent of compound statement
      - return statement as subcomponent of compound statement
      - goto statement transferring control OUTSIDE the compound statement
      - selective accept with terminate alternative
      - exit statements (default; treated as goto; override: --no-treat-exit-as-goto)
    
    KEY DIFFERENCE FROM MCCABE: Short-circuit forms (and then, or else)
    are NOT penalized as unstructured in the Ada variant.
    
    INTERPRETATION: If essential complexity > 1, the body contains unstructured
    control flow (gotos, early returns from compound statements, raises).
  
  LOOP NESTING LEVEL: Maximum depth of nested loop structures.
    Corresponds to max nesting depth restricted to loops only.
  
  EXTRA EXIT POINTS:
    count(return statements) + count(unhandled raise statements) - 1
    (for functions; the -1 accounts for the required final return)
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 22
ts: 2026-05-29T00:18:00Z
relevance: MED
title: Maintainability Index (Coleman / Oman / SEI)
authors: Don Coleman, Dan Ash, Bruce Lowther, Paul Oman (original 1994)
year: 1994
venue: IEEE Computer, Vol. 27 No. 8 (original); SEI 1997 C4 Software Technology Reference Guide
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://avandeursen.com/2014/08/29/think-twice-before-using-the-maintainability-index/
keywords: maintainability index, MI, Halstead, cyclomatic complexity, SLOC, composite metric, Visual Studio
cites: Halstead 1977, McCabe 1976, Oman-Hagemeister 1992
cited_by: Radon, Visual Studio, many composite scoring tools
content:
  ORIGINAL FORMULA (Coleman 1994):
    MI = 171 - 5.2 * ln(V) - 0.23 * G - 16.2 * ln(L)
    V = Halstead Volume
    G = total cyclomatic complexity
    L = SLOC (source lines of code)
    Range: unbounded below, up to 171
    Higher = more maintainable
  
  VISUAL STUDIO ADAPTATION:
    MI = max(0, (171 - 5.2*ln(V) - 0.23*G - 16.2*ln(L)) * 100 / 171)
    Range: 0 to 100
    Visual Studio color coding:
      Green (20-100): maintainable
      Yellow (10-19): moderate
      Red (0-9): hard to maintain
  
  RADON VARIANT (adds comment percentage):
    MI = max(0, (171 - 5.2*ln(V) - 0.23*G - 16.2*ln(L) + 50*sin(sqrt(2.46*radians(C)))) * 100 / 171)
    C = percent comment lines (0-100)
    Rank: A (> 19), B (9 < x <= 19), C (<= 9)
  
  KNOWN LIMITATIONS (per Arie van Deursen analysis):
    - Tuning constants derived from small HP proprietary dataset; not replicated.
    - Comment percentage treated as a positive contributor (questionable).
    - Composite of three measures masks which factor is causing issues.
    - Not independently validated on diverse codebases.
    - "Think twice before using" — original authors caution against blind adoption.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 23
ts: 2026-05-29T00:19:00Z
relevance: MED
title: Empirical Validation of Cognitive Complexity as Understandability Predictor
authors: Muñoz Barón, Wyrich, Wagner
year: 2020
venue: ESEM 2020 (ACM/IEEE International Symposium on Empirical Software Engineering and Measurement)
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2007.12520
keywords: cognitive complexity, cyclomatic complexity, empirical validation, understandability, human study
cites: Campbell 2018, McCabe 1976
cited_by:
content:
  STUDY DESIGN:
    216 early-career developers inspected and rated understandability of
    12 Java classes with varying Cyclomatic and Cognitive Complexity levels.
    Measured: time to understand, self-reported difficulty, correctness of
    comprehension questions.
  
  KEY FINDING:
    "Cognitive Complexity does not appear to fulfill the promise of being
    a significant improvement over previously proposed measures, as far as
    code understandability prediction is concerned."
    
    Both cognitive and cyclomatic complexity correlated similarly with
    understandability — neither was clearly superior.
    
  FOLLOW-UP STUDY (Journal of Systems and Software, 2022):
    Combining multiple metrics via Gaussian Process Regression:
    Maximum R² = 0.8742 (using multi-metric regression).
    Suggests no single metric suffices; combination works better.
  
  IMPLICATION FOR pflow:
    Neither cognitive nor cyclomatic complexity alone is a reliable proxy
    for human comprehension difficulty. Multiple metrics in combination
    provide better signal. Nesting depth and live range / DepDegree may
    add orthogonal signal to V(G) and Cognitive complexity.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 24
ts: 2026-05-29T00:20:00Z
relevance: MED
title: Slice-Based Cohesion Metrics and Refactoring
authors: Harman, Orejas, Shepperd (Binkley et al. empirical study)
year: 2007
venue: ACM TOSEM (Transactions on Software Engineering and Methodology)
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://www.cs.loyola.edu/~binkley/papers/tosem07-slice-metrics.pdf
keywords: program slice, cohesion, coupling, slice count, refactoring, complexity
cites: Weiser 1984 (program slicing)
cited_by:
content:
  SLICE-BASED COMPLEXITY METRICS:
    Basis: program slices with respect to output variables of a module.
    
    SLICE COUNT: number of distinct slices for a module (one per output variable).
      High slice count = module is doing many independent things = low cohesion.
    
    SLICE SIZE: average or max size (LOC) of slices.
      Small uniform slices = tight cohesion; large varied slices = loose.
    
    Normalized slice-based cohesion: intersection/union of slices over all
      output variable pairs.
      S_C = overlap of slices / total statements in module
      Range: 0 (no cohesion) to 1 (all statements shared across all slices)
  
  REFACTORING SIGNAL:
    Module with multiple essentially independent slices = split point.
    Slices with minimal intersection = statements in one slice that do not
    appear in another = the natural "sub-module" boundaries.
    This is a principled, data-flow-grounded way to identify Extract Method opportunities.
  
  EMPIRICAL FINDING:
    "Slice-based metrics are not proxies for simple size-based metrics (LOC)."
    They capture cohesion structure that LOC cannot.
    Longitudinal studies show slice-based metrics quantify deterioration as program ages.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 25
ts: 2026-05-29T00:21:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Spaghetti Factor: A Software Complexity Metric Proposal
authors: Philip Koopman
year: 2017
venue: betterembsw.blogspot.com (industry blog)
quality: BLOG
url: https://betterembsw.blogspot.com/2017/08/the-spaghetti-factor-software.html
keywords: spaghetti factor, global variables, cyclomatic complexity, SLOC, composite metric, embedded systems
cites: McCabe 1976
cited_by:
content:
  FORMULA:
    SF = SCC + (Globals * 5) + (SLOC / 20)
    SCC = Strict Cyclomatic Complexity (counts each boolean operator separately)
    Globals = number of read/write global variable accesses in the module
    SLOC = source lines of non-comment code
    
  DIMENSION BALANCE:
    Control flow complexity: SCC
    Data flow complexity proxy: Globals * 5 (global state = hard-to-trace deps)
    Module size: SLOC / 20
    
  THRESHOLDS:
    SF 5-10:  Sweet spot
    SF 15:    Upper limit for most modules
    SF 20:    Review for refactoring
    SF 30:    Refactor the design
    SF 50:    Untestable; redesign needed
    SF 75:    Unmaintainable; restart module
    SF 100+:  Nightmare; re-architect subsystem
  
  EXAMPLE:
    100 SLOC, SCC=9, 1 global access: SF = 9 + 5 + 5 = 19 (upper edge)
  
  NOTE: Informal/blog-level metric. Not peer-reviewed. Included for its
  practical three-dimensional perspective combining CF, DF, and size.
<<<END>>>

